###### Proof (sketch).

The Maximum Independent Set problem is the complement of the Minimum Vertex Cover problem [[12](https://arxiv.org/html/2603.07845v1#bib.bib1)]. Using the same transformation previously employed to prove the hardness of MCS and network robustness, we can transform an undirected, non-bipartite graph G′G^{\prime} into an equivalent bipartite graph GG. Computing the minimum set cover on GG is equivalent to computing the minimum vertex cover on G′G^{\prime} [[12](https://arxiv.org/html/2603.07845v1#bib.bib1)]; therefore, computing MRS on GG (with t=1t=1) is equivalent to computing the maximum independent set on G′G^{\prime}. However, unless P=NP\mathrm{P}=\mathrm{NP}, it is NP-hard to approximate the Maximum Independent Set problem to within a factor O⁡(n1−ϵ)O(n^{1-\epsilon}) for every constant ϵ>0\epsilon>0 [[33](https://arxiv.org/html/2603.07845v1#bib.bib5)]. Since MRS contains the Maximum Independent Set problem as a special case, this hardness result transfers to MRS. ∎

